@page "/getting-started"

<DocsPage Title="Installation"
          Lead="Add the packages, link their stylesheets, and put a grid on a page. A MudBlazor application adds the Wrappers and nothing else changes.">

    <DocsSection Title="Requirements">
        <ul>
            <li><b>.NET 10 or newer.</b> The packages target <code>net10.0</code> and load unchanged in newer applications.</li>
            <li><b>Chrome or Edge.</b> They are the supported browsers. Safari and Firefox are out of scope.</li>
            <li><b>An interactive render mode.</b> The grid handles keys, the pointer and the clipboard. It is verified under WebAssembly, and every browser test also runs under Blazor Server.</li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Install">
        <p>
            ExGrid and its MudBlazor Wrapper are published to nuget.org as prereleases, at one shared
            <code>0.1.0-beta.N</code> version. Upgrade the two together: the Wrapper depends on exactly
            its ExGrid version.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Path="Examples/Setup/install.sh" />
        <p>
            ExSheet, ExPivot and the data packages are built alongside ExGrid and are not part of its
            release yet. They are not on nuget.org: to use them now, reference their projects from a
            clone of the <MudLink Href="https://github.com/AkiraMisawa/ex-grid" Target="_blank">repository</MudLink>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Link the stylesheets">
        <p>
            Each package brings one stylesheet. The grid loads its own script module, so there is no
            script to add.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Path="Examples/Setup/host-page.html" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A first grid">
        <p>
            Hand the grid a source and its columns, and keep both in fields: a new source or a new
            column list on every render would render every row again.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Grid.FirstGrid)" Mud="typeof(ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Grid.FirstGridMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])" CodeOpen="true" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="In a MudBlazor application">
        <p>
            Set MudBlazor up as usual: its services, its stylesheet and script, and the theme, popover
            and dialog providers in your layout. Then link each package's stylesheet followed by its
            Wrapper's.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Path="Examples/Setup/Program.cs" />
        <CodeBlock Path="Examples/Setup/MainLayout.razor" />
        <CodeBlock Path="Examples/Setup/host-page-mud.html" />
        <p>
            Put the grid on a <code>MudExGridPaper</code> and give it <code>MudGridChrome</code>. The
            paper brings MudBlazor's surface, palette and type, and follows the theme, dark mode
            included. The Chrome fills the grid's menus, filter panel, find panel and Cell Editor with
            MudBlazor's own controls. The grid's behaviour does not change: switch the site to
            MudBlazor at the top and try the Example above again.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>
